2874755859375, -0.023101806640625, -0.017333984375, -0.035186767578125, 0.0203094482421875, -0.033233642578125, -0.0213470458984375, 0.04498291015625, -0.01316070556640625, -0.0209197998046875, 0.031829833984375, 0.0169525146484375, 0.0240936279296875, 0.0098724365234375, -0.006259918212890625, -0.019195556640625, -0.002834320068359375, -0.003536224365234375, 0.0074920654296875, 0.01192474365234375, -0.01050567626953125, 0.0005254745483398438, 0.001773834228515625, 0.0119171142578125, 0.0236968994140625, -0.00785064697265625, 0.0003261566162109375, -0.0188140869140625, 0.035614013671875, -0.00969696044921875, 0.0010652542114257812, -0.007167816162109375, 0.00478363037109375, -0.0102996826171875, 0.02667236328125, 0.0125274658203125, -0.005794525146484375, -0.0237579345703125, -0.0189971923828125, 0.0029773712158203125, -0.027374267578125, 0.00702667236328125, 0.0193023681640625, -0.0343017578125, 0.0134735107421875, 0.00765228271484375, 0.0166778564453125, 0.0003380775451660156, -0.01102447509765625, -0.01000213623046875, -0.0111236572265625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Methods**\n\n**1. Symbolic Pattern Construction and Tie Encoding**\n\n*1.1 Formal Algorithmic Implementation*  \nAt each time point, we extract each oscillator\u2019s instantaneous phase (\u03b8i) and use the network adjacency structure to identify the set of neighbors for each node. We assign a \"symbolic ordinal pattern\" by ranking \u03b8i relative to its spatial neighbors, encoding local phase ordering. Ties are handled explicitly by a defined threshold \u03b4\u03b8, whereby neighboring phase values within \u03b4\u03b8 radians are deemed indistinguishable and assigned tie notation (\u2018=\u2019).\n\n- *Tie Handling Algorithm (Pseudocode)*:  \n    ```\n    For each node i:\n        1. Collect phases: \u03b8i and \u03b8j for all j in Neigh(i)\n        2. Combine: phases_list = [\u03b8i] + [\u03b8j for j in Neigh(i)]\n        3. For each pair (a,b) in phases_list:\n             - If |\u03b8a - \u03b8b| < \u03b4\u03b8, assign tie between a and b\n        4. Assign ordinal ranks to all unique (tied) phase groupings:\n             - Same value = same rank (with '=' notation)\n             - Strictly higher phase = higher position in pattern\n        5. Output symbolic pattern as an ordered sequence, e.g., '1=2', '2 3=4', etc.\n    ```\n  - **Edge Case**: If a node has only one neighbor, ordinal encoding is reduced to a binary comparison; any ties are denoted as \u20181=2\u2019, otherwise as \u20181 2\u2019 or \u20182 1\u2019.\n\n*1.2 Rationale for Parameter Ranges and Symbolic Encoding*  \nThe selection of \u03b4\u03b8 (phase tie threshold) and neighborhood size (k) was anchored in both theoretical and empirical considerations:\n\n- **\u03b4\u03b8**: Chosen to approximate or slightly exceed the expected measurement noise and intrinsic phase fluctuation standard deviation, enabling robust identification of genuine local synchrony and plateaus. Exploratory theoretical analysis denotes that \u03b4\u03b8 should ideally be set just above the phase noise floor, but we additionally explore a practical range (see Section 2 below).\n- **k (Neighborhood Size)**: Set to match the minimal spatial context needed for meaningful phase motifs, reflecting network density. For physical sensor layouts (EEG), chosen to span direct physical adjacency, aligning with spatial signal spread; in synthetic grids/rings, set by network geometry.\n\nThis symbolic patterning enables discrimination between strictly ordered (traveling waves), tied/plateau (local synchrony), and mixed patterns, quantified as the frequency spectrum of all observed symbols.\n\n**2. Phase Difference Threshold (\u03b4\u03b8): Selection, Justification, and Procedure**\n\n*2.1 Selection Protocol*  \nTo select \u03b4\u03b8, we recommend the following procedure:\n\n1. Estimate the standard deviation of phase noise empirically or based on prior system knowledge.\n2. Sweep \u03b4\u03b8 over a plausible range (e.g., 0.05\u20130.2 radians), evaluating the stability of quantitative metrics such as spatial permutation entropy variance and classification accuracy between known dynamical regimes.\n3. Choose \u03b4\u03b8 at or slightly above the observed noise floor and within the range maximizing discrimination between dynamical regimes.\n\n*2.2 Mathematical and Conceptual Basis*  \nTheoretically, \u03b4\u03b8 should reflect the acceptably indistinguishable phase difference, trading off between noise tolerance and motif specificity:\n- If \u03c3_\u03b8_noise is the standard deviation of noise-driven phase fluctuation, then \u03b4\u03b8 \u2248 \u03c3_\u03b8_noise or slightly higher is chosen.\n- For systems where phase estimation precision is known, set \u03b4\u03b8 \u2265 max(\u03c3_\u03b8_noise, \u03c3_\u03b8_measurement).\n\n*2.3 Implementation Example*  \nAll entropy and classifier analyses for synthetic and EEG data in this study utilized \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians unless otherwise specified, supported by parameter sweeps. Full sweep results (entropy variance and classification accuracy curves) are provided in Supplementary Figure 2A\u2013B, available at [https://github.com/example-repo/spatialentropy].\n\n**3. Neighborhood Definition, Parameterization, and Edge Cases**\n\n*3.1 Synthetic Topologies (Rings, Grids)*  \n- **Neighborhood Definition**: For rings, each node has up to k nearest neighbors (default k = 2: immediate left and right). For grids, k = 4 (cardinal directions).\n- **Edge Handling**: Border nodes missing neighbors (e.g., at network edges) have sets containing only available nodes; no periodic wrapping unless stated.\n- **Expanding k**: For k > 2 (rings) or k > 4 (grids), next-nearest neighbors added in spatial order; all pattern assignments normalized for the actual neighborhood size.\n\n*3.2 EEG Networks*  \n- **Physical Layout**: For international 10-20 arrays, neighbors are channels directly adjacent on the array layout.\n- **Missing/Invalid Channels**: Nodes with missing neighbor data (e.g., bad electrodes in EEG or unconnected nodes) use available adjacent electrodes only.\n    - *Edge Case Procedure*: If only one neighbor is present, symbol encoding proceeds as binary (see Section 1.1).\n- **Irregular Layouts**: For high-density or irregular arrays, all electrodes within a radius of 4 cm (Euclidean distance in digitized 3D space) are defined as neighbors, ensuring comparable spatial coverage.\n- **Implementation**: Adjacency matrices constructed using MNE-Python 1.2 (`find_ch_adjacency`), with custom scripts (scripts and documentation: [https://github.com/example-repo/spatialentropy]; documentation specifies input file formats and function calls).\n\n*3.3 Choice and Justification of k*  \n- For EEG data, k is set to ensure each node covers ~10\u201320% of array surface area, empirically robust to 3 \u2264 k \u2264 5 (see Supplementary Figure 2C).\n- **Sensitivity Tests**: For all topologies, regime classification accuracy and entropy values remained stable (\u0394accuracy < 0.02) when varying k within typical bounds.\n\n*3.4 Irregular or Complex Networks*  \n- **Neighbor Assignment**: Proximity-based (within distance or fixed-degree); visually validated against anatomical maps as appropriate.\n- **Node Degree Normalization**: For nodes with variable neighbor counts (degree), permutation entropy and pattern counts are normalized by the number of possible unique patterns given the actual local degree, ensuring fair entropy comparison (see Section 6 for further discussion).\n\n**4. Regime Labeling, Cluster Index, and Explicit Mathematical Definitions**\n\n*Synthetic Simulations*  \n- Simulate Kuramoto oscillators (N = 100, ring or 10\u00d710 grid), varying coupling (0.05\u20131.0), frequency heterogeneity (\u03c3 = 0.02\u20130.2), noise (\u03b7 = 0.01\u20130.1) to generate target regimes.\n- Each regime consists of 50 independent simulations, T = 10,000 steps.\n\n*4.1 Regime Operational Definitions*  \n- **Synchrony**:  \n    - All \u03b8j aligned:  \n        - Kuramoto order parameter R(t) = (1/N) |\u03a3j exp(i\u03b8j)|  \n        - Mean over time window: R > 0.9\n        - Spatial phase standard deviation SD_phase < 0.1  \n- **Chimera**:  \n    - Coexistence of coherent and incoherent subgroups:  \n        - 0.5 < R < 0.7\n        - *Cluster Index* (CI) > 0.3  \n- **Traveling Wave**:  \n    - Ordered spatial phase gradient:  \n        - R \u2248 0.8\n        - Mean local |\u03b8i \u2013 \u03b8j| (for all neighbors j of i) > 0.15  \n        - Consistent monotonic phase gradient across array\n\n*4.2 Cluster Index Calculation Algorithm*  \n    ```\n    For each time t:\n        1. For each node i, identify all connected neighbor sets (size \u2265 3).\n        2. For each neighbor set S:\n             - If max(|\u03b8a\u2013\u03b8b| for all (a,b) in S) < \u03b4\u03b8, mark as a cluster.\n        3. Find size of largest such cluster: M\n        4. Compute CI(t) = M / N\n    Cluster index for a simulation is average CI(t) over analysis period.\n    ```\n    - **A node may participate in multiple clusters; assign to largest one present at each time point.\n\n*4.3 Regime Label Validation:*  \n    - All regime assignments were independently reviewed by two blinded raters (sample n = 20/regime, \u03ba = 0.92). Discrepancies were resolved by consensus.\n\n**5. Entropy Computation and Statistical Protocols**\n\n- **Windowing**: Permutation entropy was computed from symbolic patterns within overlapping time windows (250 steps, 50% overlap; shift = 125 steps).\n- **Spatial Permutation Entropy**: For each window, compute pattern frequency histogram; calculate Shannon entropy H = \u2013\u03a3 pk log pk, where pk is relative frequency of pattern k.\n- **Classifier Implementation**: Random forest (sklearn 1.1.1, 100 trees, max_depth=8, balanced class weights). Max_depth was chosen based on preliminary 5x5 grid search ([4,6,8,10,12]) to avoid overfitting\u2014chosen depth yielded best average cross-validated accuracy with \u2264 2% difference to deeper trees.\n- **Cross-Validation Protocol**: Stratified 5-fold, with all windows from a single simulation assigned to the same fold to prevent temporal leakage.\n- **Statistical Testing**:\n    - ROC and AUC statistics computed; 95% confidence intervals estimated via 1"}}